{"id":1047224,"date":"2026-09-15T19:46:11","date_gmt":"2026-09-15T23:46:11","guid":{"rendered":"https://www.nasa.gov/?p=1047224"},"modified":"2026-09-15T19:59:10","modified_gmt":"2026-09-15T23:59:10","slug":"nasa-ames-stars-of-the-month-september-2026","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https://www.nasa.gov/general/nasa-ames-stars-of-the-month-september-2026/","title":{"rendered":"NASA Ames Stars of the Month: September 2026"},"content":{"rendered":"<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1080\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?w=1920\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"NASA Ames science stars of the month for September 20206. Pictured left to right is Steve Ormsby, Aphrodite Kostakis, Alejando Borlaff, Sarah Nickerson, Martina Caussi, Keiko Nomura, and Stephen Broccardo.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg 1920w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?resize=300,169 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?resize=768,432 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?resize=1024,576 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?resize=1536,864 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?resize=400,225 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?resize=600,338 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?resize=900,506 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?resize=1200,675 1200w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1920px) 100vw, 1920px\" /></a></figure></div></div></div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The NASA Ames Science Directorate recognizes the outstanding contributions of (pictured left to right) Steve Ormsby, Aphrodite Kostakis, Alejando Borlaff, Sarah Nickerson, Martina Caussi, Keiko Nomura, and Stephen Broccardo. Their commitment to the NASA mission represents the entrepreneurial spirit, technical expertise, and collaborative disposition needed to explore this world and beyond.</p>\n\n\n\n<div style=\"height:10px\" aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-spacer\"></div>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/s-ormsby.jpg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"325\" height=\"325\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/s-ormsby.jpg?w=325\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"A portrait shot of Steve Ormsby in front of a NASA flag.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 54% 41%; object-position: 54% 41%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/s-ormsby.jpg 325w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/s-ormsby.jpg?resize=150,150 150w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/s-ormsby.jpg?resize=300,300 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/s-ormsby.jpg?resize=50,50 50w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/s-ormsby.jpg?resize=100,100 100w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/s-ormsby.jpg?resize=200,200 200w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 325px) 100vw, 325px\" /></a></figure></div></div></div>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Space Biosciences Star: Steve Ormsby&nbsp;</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Steve Ormsby is a project engineer for the Space Biosciences Division with Amentum in the Center Operations Directorate. He is recognized for his perseverance, dedication and initiative in overseeing the safety of division facilities and infrastructure maintenance, as well as exceptional diligence as a property custodian.</p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-kostakis.png\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"617\" height=\"640\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-kostakis.png?w=617\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"Portrait photo of Aphrodite Kostakis\" style=\"transform: scale(1.4); transform-origin: 49% 44%; object-position: 49% 44%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-kostakis.png 617w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-kostakis.png?resize=289,300 289w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-kostakis.png?resize=386,400 386w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/a-kostakis.png?resize=578,600 578w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 617px) 100vw, 617px\" /></a></figure></div></div></div>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Space Biosciences Star: Aphrodite Kostakis</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Aphrodite Kostakis is the Facility Service Manager with Amentum for building N239 in the Space Biosciences Division. She is acknowledged for her exemplary work ethic in maintaining building safety, preparing for and leading VPP inspections with diligence and attention, and suggesting infrastructure improvements and ensuring they get implemented for everyone’s benefit. </p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1930\" height=\"1988\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?w=1930\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"lejandro Serrano Borlaff speaks to media during the Artemis II splashdown event at Chabot Space &amp; Science Center in Oakland, California.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png 1930w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?resize=291,300 291w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?resize=768,791 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?resize=994,1024 994w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?resize=1491,1536 1491w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?resize=388,400 388w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?resize=582,600 582w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?resize=874,900 874w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/08/borlaff.png?resize=1165,1200 1165w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1930px) 100vw, 1930px\" /></a></figure></div></div></div>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Astrophysics Star: Alejandro Borlaff</strong></h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Alejandro (Alex) Borlaff, a research scientist in the Astrophysics Division with the Bay Area Environmental Research Institute, is recognized for his leadership in pioneering and developing image reduction pipelines for the Hubble Space Telescope (HST) and the Roman Space Telescope’s Wide Field Instrument (WFI). These algorithms significantly advance the detection of ultra-low surface brightness features through rigorous modeling of background contamination, including scattered and zodiacal light, better maximizing the science derived from NASA flagship astrophysics missions.&nbsp;</p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"985\" height=\"985\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?w=985\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"Portrait photo of Sarah Nickerson\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp 985w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=150,150 150w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=300,300 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=768,768 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=50,50 50w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=100,100 100w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=200,200 200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=400,400 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=600,600 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/sarah-nickerson.webp?resize=900,900 900w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 985px) 100vw, 985px\" /></a></figure></div></div></div>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Astrophysics Star: Sarah Nickerson</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Sarah Nickerson, a research scientist in the Astrophysics Division with the Bay Area Environmental Research Institute, is recognized for exceptional work on the analysis and curation of data taken from the EXES instrument on NASA’s Stratospheric Observatory for Infrared Astronomy (SOFIA) observatory. This provides high-level data products  to the global astrophysics community for continued discovery and synergy with other NASA missions.</p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1137\" height=\"1511\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg?w=1137\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"Portrait photo of Martina Caussi\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg 1137w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg?resize=226,300 226w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg?resize=768,1021 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg?resize=771,1024 771w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg?resize=301,400 301w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg?resize=451,600 451w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg?resize=677,900 677w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/martina-caussi.jpeg?resize=903,1200 903w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1137px) 100vw, 1137px\" /></a></figure></div></div></div>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Planetary Science and Exobiology Star: Martina Caussi</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Martina Caussi is a NASA Postdoctoral Program Fellow in the Planetary Science and Exobiology Division. Martina is recognized for her outstanding work using finite element simulations to determine that topographic relaxation has played a significant role in shaping Tegid, an unusually shaped impact crater on Europa. This work represents valuable precursory science in anticipation of the Europa Clipper mission, which will refine our understanding of this ocean world.</p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"1583\" height=\"2048\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?w=1583\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"Portrait of Keiko Nomura in a black shirt.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 55% 31%; object-position: 55% 31%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg 5092w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=232,300 232w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=768,994 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=791,1024 791w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=1187,1536 1187w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=1583,2048 1583w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=309,400 309w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=464,600 464w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=696,900 696w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=927,1200 927w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/acd24-0171-002.jpg?resize=1546,2000 1546w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1583px) 100vw, 1583px\" /></a></figure></div></div></div>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Earth Science Star: Keiko Nomura</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Keiko Nomura is a research scientist for the NASA Earth Exchange (NEX) in the Earth Science Division with the Bay Area Environmental Research Institute. She is acknowledged for timely efforts in leadership, stakeholder engagement, coordination with NASA HQ management, and team building for the recently funded NEX-GeoAI project. This project is now being recognized as a key step forward to establishing NASA as the ground-truthing agency for federal and private sector GeoAI modeling activities for decision making.</p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stephen_broccardo.png\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"360\" height=\"444\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stephen_broccardo.png?w=360\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 53% 45%; object-position: 53% 45%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stephen_broccardo.png 360w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stephen_broccardo.png?resize=243,300 243w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/stephen_broccardo.png?resize=324,400 324w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 360px) 100vw, 360px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\"> </div><div class=\"hds-credits\"> </div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Earth Science Star: Stephen Broccardo</h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Stephen Broccardo is a research scientist in the Earth Science Division with the Bay Area Environmental Research Institute. He is recognized for exceptional initiative and technical leadership in sustaining and advancing Ames’ atmospheric remote-sensing capabilities. Stephen has maintained critical observational capabilities&nbsp; and developed thoughtful technical milestones for future satellite-validation and science activities.</p>\n","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"<p>The NASA Ames Science Directorate recognizes the outstanding contributions of (pictured left to right) Steve Ormsby, Aphrodite Kostakis, Alejando Borlaff, Sarah Nickerson, Martina Caussi, Keiko Nomura, and Stephen Broccardo. Their commitment to the NASA mission represents the entrepreneurial spirit, technical expertise, and collaborative disposition needed to explore this world and beyond. Space Biosciences Star: Steve [&hellip;]</p>\n","protected":false},"author":452,"featured_media":1047236,"comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","sticky":false,"template":"","format":"standard","meta":{"nasa_hds_core_meta_l10n":"en-US","nasa_hds_core_meta_article_editor":"","nasa_hds_core_meta_article_contacts":[],"nasa_hds_core_meta_article_topic":"","nasa_hds_core_meta_linked_locations":[],"nasa_hds_core_meta_article_related_articles":[],"hds_migrate_meta_pr_release_id":"","hds_migrate_meta_pr_release_type":"","nasa_hds_core_meta_location_x":"","nasa_hds_core_meta_location_y":"","nasa_hds_core_meta_gallery_images":[],"nasa_hds_core_meta_excerpt_display":false,"footnotes":""},"categories":[1],"news-tags":[],"internal-terms":[],"coauthors":[12703],"class_list":["post-1047224","post","type-post","status-publish","format-standard","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","category-general"],"parsely":{"version":"1.1.0","canonical_url":"https://nasa.gov/general/nasa-ames-stars-of-the-month-september-2026/","smart_links":{"inbound":0,"outbound":0},"traffic_boost_suggestions_count":0,"meta":{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"NewsArticle","headline":"NASA Ames Stars of the Month: September 2026","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/general/nasa-ames-stars-of-the-month-september-2026/","mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.nasa.gov/general/nasa-ames-stars-of-the-month-september-2026/"},"thumbnailUrl":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?w=150&h=150&crop=1","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg"},"articleSection":"General","author":[{"@type":"Person","name":"Aaron McKinnon"}],"creator":["Aaron McKinnon"],"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"NASA","logo":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/themes/nasa/assets/images/nasa-logo.svg"},"keywords":["General"],"dateCreated":"2026-09-15T23:46:11Z","datePublished":"2026-09-15T23:46:11Z","dateModified":"2026-09-15T23:59:10Z"},"rendered":"<meta name=\"parsely-title\" content=\"NASA Ames Stars of the Month: September 2026\" />\n<meta name=\"parsely-link\" content=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/nasa-ames-stars-of-the-month-september-2026/\" />\n<meta name=\"parsely-type\" content=\"post\" />\n<meta name=\"parsely-image-url\" content=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg?w=150&amp;h=150&amp;crop=1\" />\n<meta name=\"parsely-pub-date\" content=\"2026-09-15T23:46:11Z\" />\n<meta name=\"parsely-section\" content=\"General\" />\n<meta name=\"parsely-tags\" content=\"General\" />\n<meta name=\"parsely-author\" content=\"Aaron McKinnon\" />","tracker_url":"https://cdn.parsely.com/keys/nasa.gov/p.js"},"read_time":3,"time_ago":"4 weeks","publish_date":"September 15, 2026","featured_image_url":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg","featured_image":{"id":1047236,"file":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026.jpg","title":"nasa-arc-s-stars-of-the-mont-sep-2026","alt":"NASA Ames science stars of the month for September 20206. Pictured left to right is Steve Ormsby, Aphrodite Kostakis, Alejando Borlaff, Sarah Nickerson, Martina Caussi, Keiko Nomura, and Stephen Broccardo.","caption":"NASA Ames science stars of the month for September 20206. Pictured left to right is Steve Ormsby, Aphrodite Kostakis, Alejando Borlaff, Sarah Nickerson, Martina Caussi, Keiko Nomura, and Stephen Broccardo."},"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/1047224","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/posts"}],"about":[{"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/types/post"}],"author":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/users/452"}],"replies":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/comments?post=1047224"}],"version-history":[{"count":7,"href":"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/1047224/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":104724